<section xml:id="sec-mod6-worked-examples">
  <title>Worked Examples</title>
  <p>Estimated time: 40 minutes.</p>
    <p>
      Read each example slowly. The goal is not just to see what the
      answer is, but to internalize the proof <em>template</em>. <xref ref="ex-mod6-we-1"/>
      is the archetype of an <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>\delta</m> limit
      proof; <xref ref="ex-mod6-we-2"/> shows how to prove continuity at every point of
      <m>\R</m> using the same method; <xref ref="ex-mod6-we-3"/> demonstrates the
      sequential criterion for proving non-uniform continuity; <xref ref="ex-mod6-we-4"/>
      shows the Lipschitz method for proving uniform continuity on a
      bounded interval.
    </p>
  <!-- ============================================================
       EXAMPLE 1: EPSILON-DELTA LIMIT PROOF
  ============================================================ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-mod6-we-1">
    <title>An <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>\delta</m> Limit Proof</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that <m>\lim_{x \to 2} (3x - 1) = 5</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Strategy.</alert>
        We use the <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>\delta</m> definition directly.
        The proof has two phases: scratch work to <em>find</em>
        <m>\delta</m> in terms of <m>\varepsilon</m>, and a formal
        proof that <em>verifies</em> that choice works.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Scratch work.</alert>
        We need <m>|f(x) - 5| \lt \varepsilon</m> when
        <m>0 \lt |x - 2| \lt \delta</m>. Compute:
        <md>|f(x) - 5| = |(3x - 1) - 5| = |3x - 6| = 3|x - 2|.</md>
        For this to be less than <m>\varepsilon</m>, we need
        <m>3|x - 2| \lt \varepsilon</m>, i.e., <m>|x - 2| \lt \varepsilon / 3</m>.
        Set <m>\delta = \varepsilon / 3</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Proof.</alert>
        Let <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>. Set <m>\delta = \varepsilon / 3</m>.
        Suppose <m>0 \lt |x - 2| \lt \delta</m>. Then
        <md>|f(x) - 5| = |(3x - 1) - 5| = 3|x - 2| \lt 3\delta = \varepsilon.</md>
        By the definition of limit, <m>\lim_{x \to 2} (3x - 1) = 5</m>.
        <m>\square</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>What to notice.</alert>
        The scratch work determines <m>\delta</m>; the formal proof
        simply verifies the chain of inequalities. This two-phase
        structure is the standard template for all
        <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>\delta</m> proofs. Notice that in this
        linear example the expression <m>|f(x) - L|</m> simplified to
        a constant times <m>|x - a|</m> immediately. In <xref ref="ex-mod6-we-2"/>, the
        function is quadratic and the proof needs an extra step to bound the
        remaining factor.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ============================================================
       EXAMPLE 2: CONTINUITY AT EVERY POINT
  ============================================================ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-mod6-we-2">
    <title>Proving Continuity Using the <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>\delta</m> Definition</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that <m>f(x) = x^2</m> is continuous at every
        <m>a \in \R</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Strategy.</alert>
        We must show <m>|x^2 - a^2| \lt \varepsilon</m> whenever
        <m>|x - a| \lt \delta</m>. The key is to factor
        <m>|x^2 - a^2| = |x - a| \cdot |x + a|</m> and bound
        <m>|x + a|</m> by first restricting <m>|x - a| \lt 1</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Scratch work.</alert>
        Restrict <m>|x - a| \lt 1</m> first. Then
        <m>|x| \lt |a| + 1</m>, so
        <m>|x + a| \leq |x| + |a| \lt 2|a| + 1</m>. Therefore
        <md>|x^2 - a^2| = |x - a| \cdot |x + a| \lt (2|a| + 1) \cdot |x - a|.</md>
        For this to be less than <m>\varepsilon</m>, we need
        <m>|x - a| \lt \varepsilon / (2|a| + 1)</m>. Set
        <m>\delta = \min(1,\, \varepsilon / (2|a| + 1))</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Proof.</alert>
        Let <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>. Set
        <m>\delta = \min(1,\, \varepsilon / (2|a| + 1))</m>. Suppose
        <m>|x - a| \lt \delta</m>. Since <m>\delta \leq 1</m>, we have
        <m>|x - a| \lt 1</m>, which gives <m>|x| \lt |a| + 1</m> and
        hence <m>|x + a| \leq |x| + |a| \lt 2|a| + 1</m>. Therefore
        <md>|x^2 - a^2| = |x - a| \cdot |x + a|
        \lt \delta \cdot (2|a| + 1)
        \leq \frac{\varepsilon}{2|a| + 1} \cdot (2|a| + 1) = \varepsilon.</md>
        Since <m>a \in \R</m> was arbitrary, <m>f(x) = x^2</m> is
        continuous at every <m>a \in \R</m>.
        <m>\square</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>What to notice.</alert>
        The <q>restrict to a neighborhood first</q> step is the
        essential technical move in continuity proofs for nonlinear
        functions. By requiring <m>|x - a| \lt 1</m>, you get a bound
        on <m>|x + a|</m> that does not blow up. Note that <m>\delta</m>
        depends on both <m>\varepsilon</m> and <m>a</m>; this is
        perfectly fine for <em>continuity</em> (pointwise). The same
        function <m>x^2</m> is <em>not</em> uniformly continuous on
        <m>\R</m>, but this proof does not show that. The growth of
        <m>2|a| + 1</m> as <m>|a| \to \infty</m> shows only that
        <em>this</em> choice of <m>\delta</m> shrinks as <m>|a|</m>
        grows, not that every choice must. To prove that no single
        <m>\delta</m> works for all points, you need the sequential
        criterion (<xref ref="ex-mod6-sg-34-2"/>, used in
        <xref ref="ex-mod6-we-3"/>);
        you will carry it out in <xref ref="ex-mod6-ps-s4"/>.
        On any bounded interval, however,
        <m>2|a| + 1</m> is bounded above, and uniform continuity
        follows (see <xref ref="ex-mod6-we-4"/>).
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ============================================================
       EXAMPLE 3: NOT UNIFORMLY CONTINUOUS
  ============================================================ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-mod6-we-3">
    <title>Proving <m>f(x) = 1/x</m> Is Not Uniformly Continuous on <m>(0,1)</m></title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that <m>f(x) = 1/x</m> is not uniformly continuous on
        <m>(0, 1)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Strategy.</alert>
        To negate uniform continuity, we must exhibit <m>\varepsilon_0 \gt 0</m>
        such that for every <m>\delta \gt 0</m>, there exist
        <m>x, y \in (0, 1)</m> with <m>|x - y| \lt \delta</m> but
        <m>|f(x) - f(y)| \geq \varepsilon_0</m>. The cleanest way is
        the sequential criterion of <xref ref="ex-mod6-sg-34-2"/>:
        produce explicit sequences <m>(x_n)</m> and <m>(y_n)</m> in
        <m>(0, 1)</m> with <m>|x_n - y_n| \to 0</m> but
        <m>|f(x_n) - f(y_n)| \geq \varepsilon_0</m> for all <m>n</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Proof.</alert>
        Let <m>x_n = 1/n</m> and <m>y_n = 1/(n + 1)</m> for
        <m>n \geq 2</m>. Both sequences lie in <m>(0, 1)</m> and we
        compute
        <md>|x_n - y_n| = \frac{1}{n} - \frac{1}{n+1} = \frac{1}{n(n+1)} \to 0.</md>
        However,
        <md>|f(x_n) - f(y_n)| = |n - (n + 1)| = 1</md>
        for all <m>n \geq 2</m>. Therefore <m>|f(x_n) - f(y_n)| = 1
        \not\to 0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        To make the argument formal: take <m>\varepsilon_0 = 1/2</m>.
        For any <m>\delta \gt 0</m>, choose <m>n</m> large enough
        that <m>1/(n(n+1)) \lt \delta</m>. Then <m>x_n, y_n \in (0,1)</m>
        satisfy <m>|x_n - y_n| \lt \delta</m> but
        <m>|f(x_n) - f(y_n)| = 1 \geq \varepsilon_0</m>. Since
        <m>\delta \gt 0</m> was arbitrary, no single <m>\delta</m> works
        for this <m>\varepsilon_0</m>, and <m>f</m> is not uniformly
        continuous on <m>(0, 1)</m>.
        <m>\square</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>What to notice.</alert>
        The negation of uniform continuity
        (<m>\exists \varepsilon_0 \gt 0\ \forall \delta \gt 0\ \exists x, y \in S:
        |x - y| \lt \delta \text{ and } |f(x) - f(y)| \geq \varepsilon_0</m>)
        is most cleanly verified with an explicit pair of sequences.
        The key is that <m>x_n</m> and <m>y_n</m> both approach 0
        (where <m>1/x</m> blows up), so the function values diverge
        even though the inputs become arbitrarily close.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ============================================================
       EXAMPLE 4: UNIFORM CONTINUITY VIA LIPSCHITZ
  ============================================================ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-mod6-we-4">
    <title>Uniform Continuity via a Lipschitz Condition</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that <m>f(x) = x^2</m> is uniformly continuous on
        <m>[0, 2]</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Strategy.</alert>
        On the closed bounded interval <m>[0, 2]</m>, we can bound
        <m>|x + y|</m> uniformly for all <m>x, y \in [0, 2]</m>. This
        gives a Lipschitz condition <m>|f(x) - f(y)| \leq K|x - y|</m>
        with a <em>fixed</em> constant <m>K</m>, which immediately
        implies uniform continuity with <m>\delta = \varepsilon / K</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Proof.</alert>
        For any <m>x, y \in [0, 2]</m>,
        <md>|f(x) - f(y)| = |x^2 - y^2| = |x + y| \cdot |x - y|.</md>
        Since <m>x, y \in [0, 2]</m>, we have
        <m>|x + y| \leq |x| + |y| \leq 2 + 2 = 4</m>. Therefore
        <md>|f(x) - f(y)| \leq 4|x - y|.</md>
        This is a Lipschitz condition with constant <m>K = 4</m>.
        Given <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>, set <m>\delta = \varepsilon / 4</m>.
        Then for all <m>x, y \in [0, 2]</m> with <m>|x - y| \lt \delta</m>,
        <md>|f(x) - f(y)| \leq 4|x - y| \lt 4\delta = \varepsilon.</md>
        Since the same <m>\delta</m> works for all <m>x, y \in [0, 2]</m>,
        <m>f</m> is uniformly continuous on <m>[0, 2]</m>.
        <m>\square</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>What to notice.</alert>
        The Lipschitz constant <m>K = 4</m> comes from the global
        bound <m>|x + y| \leq 4</m> on <m>[0, 2]</m>. On all of
        <m>\R</m>, no such global bound exists (<m>|x + y|</m> can be
        made arbitrarily large), so this argument does not extend to
        <m>\R</m>. That failure alone does not prove that <m>x^2</m> is
        not uniformly continuous on <m>\R</m>, because a function can be
        uniformly continuous without satisfying any Lipschitz condition
        (an example follows). The proof that <m>x^2</m> is not uniformly
        continuous on <m>\R</m> uses the sequential criterion
        (<xref ref="ex-mod6-sg-34-2"/>, used in
        <xref ref="ex-mod6-we-3"/>);
        see <xref ref="ex-mod6-ps-s4"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        A Lipschitz condition is sufficient for uniform continuity, but
        not necessary. The Heine<ndash/>Cantor Theorem says that every
        continuous function on a closed bounded interval <m>[a, b]</m>
        is uniformly continuous there; it does not say that such a
        function satisfies a Lipschitz condition. The proof of
        Heine<ndash/>Cantor is a topic for MAT 5610. To see the
        difference, consider
        <m>f(x) = \sqrt{x}</m>: it is continuous on <m>[0, 1]</m>, so it is
        uniformly continuous there, but it satisfies no Lipschitz
        condition on <m>[0, 1]</m>: for <m>0 \lt x \leq 1</m>,
        <md>|\sqrt{x} - \sqrt{0}| = \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\, |x - 0|,</md>
        and <m>1/\sqrt{x} \gt K</m> as soon as <m>x \lt 1/K^2</m>, so no
        constant <m>K</m> works for every pair of points. (In
        <xref ref="ex-mod6-as-2"/> you will prove directly that
        <m>\sqrt{x}</m> is uniformly continuous on all of
        <m>[0, \infty)</m>.) A stronger hypothesis does give a Lipschitz
        condition: if <m>f</m> is differentiable on <m>[a, b]</m> and
        <m>f'</m> is continuous there, then the Extreme Value Theorem
        applied to <m>|f'|</m> gives a constant <m>K</m> with
        <m>|f'(t)| \leq K</m> for all <m>t \in [a, b]</m>. If
        <m>x, y \in [a, b]</m> and <m>x \neq y</m>, the Mean Value
        Theorem (<xref ref="ch-mod7"/>) gives
        a point <m>c</m> between them with
        <m>|f(x) - f(y)| = |f'(c)|\,|x - y| \leq K|x - y|</m>; if
        <m>x = y</m>, the inequality holds trivially.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

</section>
